<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">SchUM cities</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ShUM cities</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The SchUM cities are Speyer, Worms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, whose Jewish communities formed a federation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and were leading centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> learning. The name comes from the initial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> letters of the cities' names. Their synagogues, ritual baths and cemeteries, among them the Holy Sand in Worms, were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2021.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schum-cities.jpg" alt="SchUM cities" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob984 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EU-Germany_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
